Let $\mu$ be a positive Borel measure on the open unit disc $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$, and consider the Hilbert space $L^2(\mathbb{D},d\mu)$ of square-integrable complex-valued functions with respect to $\mu$. Let $\bar\partial:L^2(\mathbb{D},d\mu)\to L^2(\mathbb{D},d\mu)$ denote the Cauchy–Riemann operator (acting in the distributional sense on $(0,0)$-forms with values in $(0,1)$-forms), and let $\bar\partial^*$ denote its Hilbert space adjoint with respect to $d\mu$. The associated complex Laplacian (Kohn Laplacian) is $\Box_\mu=\bar\partial\,\bar\partial^*+\bar\partial^*\bar\partial$.

Develop a solution theory for the $\bar\partial$-Neumann problem in this weighted setting. Specifically, determine conditions on the measure $\mu$ under which:
(i) the operator $\Box_\mu$ has closed range and a bounded inverse (the $\bar\partial$-Neumann operator $N_\mu$) on the orthogonal complement of its kernel;
(ii) one obtains existence, uniqueness, and regularity results for the canonical solution $u=\bar\partial^* N_\mu f$ of the equation $\bar\partial u=f$ for $f\in L^2(\mathbb{D},d\mu)$ with $\bar\partial f=0$;
(iii) compactness, subellipticity, or other regularity properties of $N_\mu$ hold, in terms of analytic properties of $d\mu$.

The goal is a systematic theory of the $\bar\partial$-Neumann problem in $L^2(d\mu)$ for general measures $\mu$ on the unit disc.

