Let (M, g) be a smooth, time-oriented, connected Lorentzian 4-manifold (a spacetime) satisfying the Einstein field equations. Call (M, g) extensible if there exists a smooth isometric embedding ι : M ↪ (M̃, g̃) into a strictly larger connected Lorentzian 4-manifold (M̃, g̃) such that ι(M) ≠ M̃. An extension (M̃, g̃) is called analytic if both (M, g) and (M̃, g̃) are real-analytic and ι is an analytic isometric embedding. Assume that (M, g) contains an inextendible causal curve γ : [0, a) → M of finite generalized affine parameter ("incomplete curve") along which all polynomial curvature scalars built from g, its inverse, and the Riemann tensor remain bounded (no curvature pathology).

Formulate and justify a general criterion that determines:
(a) whether such an extensible spacetime (M, g) must be extended;
(b) by what mathematical and physical principles the choice of extension should be made;
(c) why such an extension is required (i.e., what role does the incomplete curve play in mandating it);
(d) to which larger spacetime (M̃, g̃) it should be extended.

The criterion must address the following obstructions:
(i) extensions can be non-obvious, i.e., not detectable from local geometric data near the end of γ;
(ii) extensions can be non-unique even within the smooth category;
(iii) extensions can be non-unique even within the real-analytic category;
(iv) no extension may exist at all, even though γ is incomplete and no curvature scalar diverges along it.

Provide either a well-defined construction selecting a canonical extension (or a canonical class of extensions) from (M, g), or a proof that no such canonical selection exists in full generality.

