Let $K$ be a finite graph (1-dimensional simplicial complex) and let $f:K\to\mathbb{R}^2$ be a piecewise-linear map. Call $f$ approximable by embeddings if for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists a piecewise-linear embedding $g:K\hookrightarrow\mathbb{R}^2$ with $\sup_{x\in K}\|g(x)-f(x)\|<\varepsilon$.

The van Kampen obstruction $\theta(f)\in\mathbb{Z}/2$ is defined as follows. Perturb $f$ to a piecewise-linear map $f':K\to\mathbb{R}^2$ in general position, so that the only singularities of $f'$ are finitely many transverse double points. For each unordered pair $\{\sigma,\tau\}$ of disjoint (closed) edges of $K$, count the number of intersection points of $f'(\sigma)$ and $f'(\tau)$, and sum these counts modulo $2$ over all such pairs. The result is independent of the chosen perturbation and is denoted $\theta(f)$.

It is classical that if $f$ is approximable by embeddings then $\theta(f)=0$. Prove the converse — that $\theta(f)=0$ implies $f$ is approximable by embeddings — in each of the following three cases:

(a) $K$ is an arbitrary finite graph and $f$ is monotone, meaning that the preimage $f^{-1}(y)$ is connected for every $y\in\mathbb{R}^2$;

(b) $K$ is an arc (i.e., homeomorphic to $[0,1]$);

(c) $K$ is a tree.

